## FINDING 1 -- an hourly miner has fetched a 404 for 79 consecutive runs, and the module's own
## fail-soft contract is what made it invisible

`desks/mt5/side_channels/seed_miners.py::mine_fxblue` fetches `https://www.fxblue.com/users`.
That URL is a 404 and has been throughout the retained window:

    79 discoveries_*.json files, 2026-08-27T00:22 -> 2026-08-30T06:22
    79 rows total, kind=fetch_error, 79/79
    single cause: "404 Client Error: Not Found for url: https://www.fxblue.com/users"
    rows of real content, ever: ZERO

**The mechanism of invisibility is the interesting part and it generalises to all sixteen Seed
Drop 2 miners.** The module's stated contract is that a source whose page shape drifts "degrades
to raw-capture rows flagged `needs_selector_work` -- corpus either way". That is a good contract
for a DRIFTED SELECTOR and a fatal one for a DEAD ENDPOINT: both render as a non-empty
discoveries file with a `needs_selector_work` flag, so a file arriving every hour reads as a
healthy channel to anything counting files or bytes. A 404 is not a selector problem and no
amount of selector iteration will fix it. This is the desk's `health = usable output` rule
(gap-wirer e) arriving one layer too late.

**The route was already on disk, in this desk's own tree, unadopted.**
`desks/mt5/scripts/fxblue_track_record_miner.py` documents at the top of the file that FX Blue
offers NO population route of its own (its sitemap lists exactly one user, `/users/example`) and
that the working enumerator is the Wayback CDX URL index -- 5,076 handles, already cached at
`desks/mt5/data/intelligence/fxblue/population.txt`. The hourly miner was reaching for a
directory page that the deep miner had already established does not exist.

**REPAIR (applied, verified live):** `mine_fxblue` now reads the cached population and emits 40
`track_record` rows per run against the working `api.fxblue.com/wl/view.aspx` route, rotating the
window by the hour so successive runs point at different ground (L1.61 -- coverage is a cycle,
not a sweep). Verified: 40 rows, first `achab-fxg93`, last `airhopper_forex`. 79 runs of zero
became 40 rows/hour. If the population cache is ever absent or empty the miner now says so as a
`fetch_error` with the repair named, rather than as a selector complaint.

## FINDING 2 -- the twice-deferred timer, built and armed

The productive miner had harvested **1,056 of 5,077 handles (20.8%)** across seven manual waves
and had no schedule. Every previous log asked for a timer and every previous run did the harvest
by hand.

**REPAIR (applied, running):**
- Added `--cursor-file` to `fxblue_track_record_miner.py`: a persistent position into a
  deterministic **stride-7 interleave** of the population. The lanes are concatenated, so the
  slice at any cursor is block-SPREAD (the population is block-structured -- contiguous
  bulk-registered `22-*` runs, which is why a contiguous slice measures one block rather than
  the population) while the cycle still reaches every handle exactly once and then laps.
- The cursor advances **only after publication**. A cursor advanced before the write turns a
  crashed run into permanently skipped ground -- a silent coverage hole (L1.51), and the exact
  shape of failure this ground has already produced once.
- `ops/quant-fxblue-harvest.{service,timer}`, installed and `enable --now`: 60 handles every 2
  hours -> the full 5,077 population in ~7 days, then laps onto newer data forever.

Verified live, not asserted: first service run `cursor: 0/5077`, `20/60 data=7 shell=13 dead=0
failed=0` -- real records, not shells-only. Next scheduled fire 08:19 UTC.

## FINDING 3 -- 75.3% of the practitioner corpus handed to the extractor is JavaScript, and the
## truncation cap means the best pages lose their prose entirely

`data/intelligence/gpt_practitioner_corpus.jsonl` is 4.0 MB and grows hourly. Measured:

    430 rows
    rows with any `text`:            0
    total `text` chars in the file:  0
    share of file bytes in `description`: 92%  (3,782,649 of ~4.1M)

The 262 youtube rows are the known-dead transcript route (104 `captionTracks absent`, 90
zero-byte caption bodies, 68 HTTP 429) and are correctly recorded as failures. The other 183
rows -- `site`, `atom`, `x` -- carry their content in `description`, and that is where the defect
is: `libs/research/public_strategy_hunter.py` built it with

    text = re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", payload.decode(...))

which strips the `<script>` and `<style>` TAGS and keeps everything BETWEEN them. Measured over
the 183 rows: **2,751,623 of 3,652,268 description chars (75.3%) are JavaScript-shaped**, and
**51 of 183 rows hit the 50,000-char cap**. Head-of-document scripts come FIRST, so on precisely
the richest pages the cap was spent on the bundle and the prose was truncated away. The
extractor was not reading a page; it was reading a build artifact.

**REPAIR (applied, measured against the live page):** strip `<script>/<style>/<noscript>/
<template>` BODIES and HTML comments before the tag strip. On `man.com/maninstitute`:

    before: "window.dataLayer = window.dataLayer || []; window.dataLayer.push({"traffic_t":..."
    after:  "Man Insights | Man Group Funds Funds Fund centre Investor resources Document..."

The character count only falls 25,465 -> 20,571 on that page, which is the point worth keeping:
the win is not volume, it is that the first 50,000 chars are now content. A byte count would have
scored this repair as a small regression.

## CONVERGENCE

Findings 1 and 3 are the same defect in two costumes and I am recording that rather than counting
them as two independent discoveries: **a producer emitting a well-formed artifact that contains
none of the thing it exists to collect**, certified healthy by file arrival (1) or by byte growth
(3). Neither would fail any emptiness check the desk currently runs. The generalisable test is
the one already in the desk's own vocabulary -- health is USABLE OUTPUT -- and neither channel
had an instrument for it.

